From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HeDo <ldileo@digipa.it>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 18:57:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] BitStream::reserve frees the buffer it replaces, and
 allocates nothing for zero words

reserve() allocated the new buffer and recorded its size in `allocated`, which is also the flag
that says the stream owns its buffer (the destructor deletes only when it is non-zero). So
reserve(0), which Encoder::encodeMesh() calls for a node of fewer than 4 vertices
(nvert / 4 == 0), allocated an empty array that no destructor freed, and the first write()
then saw `!allocated`, called reserve(256) and dropped the empty array: a leak of one
allocation for every such node (LeakSanitizer: "Direct leak ... crt::BitStream::reserve").

reserve() now deletes the buffer it owns before it allocates, and does not allocate for zero
words: the stream stays without a buffer, and write() reserves its 256 words at the first
write, as it did. A stream that holds a buffer it does not own (init(), for reading) is not
deleted. BitStream(int) starts with allocated = 0, because it never set it before it called
reserve(). The bytes written do not change.
---
 src/bitstream.cpp | 6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/bitstream.cpp b/src/bitstream.cpp
index 1b85303..b5ca4ea 100644
--- a/src/bitstream.cpp
+++ b/src/bitstream.cpp
@@ -44,7 +44,7 @@ static uint32_t bmask[] = {
 
 //TODO is it faster using bmask or using ~((1L<<d)-1)?
 
-BitStream::BitStream(int reserved) { //for write
+BitStream::BitStream(int reserved): allocated(0) { //for write
 	reserve(reserved);
 
 
@@ -70,8 +70,10 @@ void BitStream::init(int _size, uint32_t *_buffer) { //in uint32_t units for rea
 }
 
 void BitStream::reserve(int reserved) { //in uint32_t units for reading
+	if(allocated)
+		delete []buffer;
 	allocated = reserved;
-	pos = buffer = new uint32_t[allocated];
+	buffer = allocated ? new uint32_t[allocated] : 0;
 	size = 0;
 	buff = 0;
 	bits = BITS_PER_WORD;
